WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.049
Film Courage: Gary, I sense from you such
an open, abundance mindset.

00:00:04.049 --> 00:00:05.170
Did you always think like this?

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:07.580
Have you always felt like things were unlimited?

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:12.400
That there was no…a lot of us just think
in terms of a box (myself included).

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:14.830
And I don’t see that from you.

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:19.721
I sense that you see endless possibilities
and there’s never a black-and-white answer,

00:00:19.721 --> 00:00:20.850
there’s a lot of grey.

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:24.090
Gary W. Goldstein: My world is entirely grey,
there is no black-and-white.

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:27.400
And it is about endless possibility.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:29.210
It’s what you make of it.

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:35.730
And I came to Hollywood wide-eyed, with rose-colored
glasses and by the way, I am still wearing

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:36.780
rose-colored glasses.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:41.510
I will be the eternal optimist until the day
I leave this life because it’s a better

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:43.739
choice for me.

00:00:43.739 --> 00:00:47.210
But when someone says no…the thing about
moving to Los Angeles to try and get in the

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:49.510
film business, they spent years getting “No.”

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:53.340
A lot of “no’s,” hundreds of “no’s,”
thousands of “no’s.”

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:57.110
And a very rare instance, I might get a “yes.”

00:00:57.110 --> 00:00:58.960
But the “no’s” still outweighed it.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:06.420
It’s interesting, there was a book by Jeff
Olson called The Slight Edge and in that book

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:12.270
he talks about social science studies have
proven that a child by the age of 5 has heard

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:15.020
the word “no” 40,000 times.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:18.189
And that same child has heard the word “yes”
8,000 times.

00:01:18.189 --> 00:01:22.490
40,000 “no’s” - 8,000 times of “yes.”

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:29.349
5 times the gravity holding that child down
as the buoyant “yes” uplifting this child’s

00:01:29.349 --> 00:01:32.850
mindset and sense of possibility.

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:34.890
That’s a formative time.

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:41.969
So they enter the first grade with that energy.

00:01:41.969 --> 00:01:43.909
Quite a deficit there.

00:01:43.909 --> 00:01:53.659
Imagine the possibility if that were reversed,
how would that kid enter into his social environments?

00:01:53.659 --> 00:01:57.630
But be that as it may, I see the world as
endless possibility.

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:01.079
When someone says “no” to me, I’ve hear
it so many times that I’ve gone deaf.

00:02:01.079 --> 00:02:03.299
I am literally deaf to the word.

00:02:03.299 --> 00:02:06.299
So to me, when you say “no” to me, I’m
smiling and I’m excited.

00:02:06.299 --> 00:02:09.069
Why, because it’s the same as “yes.”

00:02:09.069 --> 00:02:12.019
I choose it to be the same as “yes.”

00:02:12.019 --> 00:02:13.510
Why?

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:15.640
I know that “no” is not “yes.”

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:20.140
But what I do know is that the greater currency,
whatever you are going to say yes to, it would

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:23.100
have been nice, it would have made me happy.

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:29.060
But the real win is my relationship to you,
with you.

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:35.140
So if I can take that no and honor by saying
fantastic.

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:37.900
First of all, I’ve taken all, you know people
don’t like to say no.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:40.610
It makes them feel terrible and they feel
guilty and this and that.

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:43.709
But if I take that away right off the table
by going “Oh, my Gosh!

00:02:43.709 --> 00:02:44.709
Great.”

00:02:44.709 --> 00:02:49.470
and surprise them, well new rules, right?

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:51.790
So I can say to them “that’s fabulous.”

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:57.790
You have no idea the value you would bring
to my life that you would honor me if you

00:02:57.790 --> 00:03:01.140
would be my 3.7 minute mentor right now.

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:03.150
My 5 minute mentor.

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:09.950
If you can share with me why this is a “no”
to you, I would be eternally grateful.

00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:13.810
Now you’ve opened the door to them telling
you the truth.

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:14.940
They are not going to say “no.”

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:18.209
You’ve flattered them, you’ve honored
them, you’ve made them smile, hopefully

00:03:18.209 --> 00:03:22.760
made them laugh and you’re being honest
and they know that.

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:25.139
We know when someone is being honest with
us.

00:03:25.139 --> 00:03:32.129
And now out comes the thing called empathy
and that thing called caring, so they’ll

00:03:32.129 --> 00:03:33.639
tell you.

00:03:33.639 --> 00:03:39.629
And A) you will learn something important
and B)

00:03:39.629 --> 00:03:41.379
That ineffable thing happens.

00:03:41.379 --> 00:03:46.580
We as two people have just bonded in an entirely
higher context.

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:50.219
I’ve left the room with “no” and a friend.

00:03:50.219 --> 00:03:55.519
A “no” and a closer relationship and a
new mentor.

00:03:55.519 --> 00:04:01.310
You do that a hundred times over the course
of a couple of years, success is inevitable

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:05.560
because people who care about you, who are
more successful will ensure that.

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:08.069
That’s the way it works.

00:04:08.069 --> 00:04:13.290
Film Courage: Have you had a “no” come
back to you a year or two or three later (whatever)

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:14.489
and say “You know what?

00:04:14.489 --> 00:04:16.389
Gary, remember we talked…”

00:04:16.389 --> 00:04:30.250
There was no negativity, you kept it upbeat
and it turned out there was something else

00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:31.540
they thought of you for?

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:36.229
Gary W. Goldstein: How you approach people
determines how they feel about you, not just

00:04:36.229 --> 00:04:39.090
in the moment but ongoing.

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:44.810
I have had a lot of people over time come
back with opportunity in a different form,

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:51.069
a different time, a different place, a different
topic, a different project if you will.

00:04:51.069 --> 00:04:58.770
And invite me into a conversation that was
largely the result of earlier interactions

00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:02.820
and those interactions left them with a positive
feeling.

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:06.900
I didn’t make them feel bad, quite the opposite.

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:13.110
And that’s what success looks like to me.

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:19.620
Success looks like a string of pearls, human
relationships that were dealt with well in

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:25.650
the moment because now people are talking
about me behind my back, but in a good way

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:33.250
or thinking about me perhaps in a better way
and then remembering me because of it.

00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:37.430
And I think we all do that.

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:44.139
We like to do business with people that we
like, that we respect, that we trust, that

00:05:44.139 --> 00:05:45.330
we know how they’re going to behave.

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:50.379
If I deliver bad news to you and come back
in a positive way to me, I know that you’re

00:05:50.379 --> 00:05:53.449
the writer I want in a writer room at 3:00
a.m. in the morning when things are really

00:05:53.449 --> 00:05:59.360
tough and getting ugly and I can count on
you to still be a human being.

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:01.970
That does matter.

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:07.569
You will always get hired over the slightly
more talented writer if I don’t trust that

00:06:07.569 --> 00:06:11.630
to be true about them, but I do trust that
to be true about you.

00:06:11.630 --> 00:06:13.029
That’s how we’re wired.

